  <!-- API mode page -->
  <data name="ApiModePageTitle.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>API mode</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ApiModePageDescription.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The API mode decides which MIDI stack Windows uses. Changing it needs a restart.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ApiModeLearnMoreLink.Content" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Read about the API modes</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ApiModeServicesName.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>0 - Windows MIDI Services</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ApiModeServicesDescription.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The default. Multi-client MIDI, loopback endpoints, Network MIDI 2.0, Bluetooth LE MIDI, MIDI 2.0 devices and the combined MIDI 1.0 and MIDI 2.0 class driver.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ApiModeLegacyName.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>1 - Legacy MIDI</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ApiModeLegacyDescription.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>The MIDI stack as it was before Windows MIDI Services. No multi-client access, no built-in loopbacks, no Network MIDI 2.0 and no MIDI 2.0 devices. Choose this if you have an application, driver, or device incompatibility with Windows MIDI Services, or you need to use legacy WinMM user-mode drivers (typically ".drv"), or DirectMusic drivers.</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ApiModeHybridName.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>2 - Hybrid</value>
  </data>
  <data name="ApiModeHybridDescription.Text" xml:space="preserve">
    <value>Not recommended for most customers. Devices on a MIDI 1.0 driver appear only to the old APIs, and devices on the new class driver only to the new API. The two do not see each other.</value>
  </data>
